  <subsection title="Translate Phrases to Expressions with Fractions">
    <p>The words quotient and ratio are often used to describe fractions. In Subtract Whole Numbers, we defined quotient as the result of division. The quotient of $$a$$ and $$b$$ is the result you get from dividing $$a$$ by $$b,$$ or $$\frac{a}{b}.$$ Let’s practice translating some phrases into algebraic expressions using these terms.</p>
    <example number="4.41">
      <problem>
        <p>Translate the phrase into an algebraic expression: “the quotient of $$3x$$ and $$8\operatorname{."}$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>The keyword is quotient; it tells us that the operation is division. Look for the words of and and to find the numbers to divide.</p>
        <equation>$$\text{The quotient}\ \text{of}\ 3x\ \text{and}\ 8.$$</equation>
        <p>This tells us that we need to divide $$3x$$ by $$8.$$ $$\frac{3x}{8}$$</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.81">
      <p>Translate the phrase into an algebraic expression: the quotient of $$9s$$ and $$14.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.82">
      <p>Translate the phrase into an algebraic expression: the quotient of $$5y$$ and $$6.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="4.42">
      <problem>
        <p>Translate the phrase into an algebraic expression: the quotient of the difference of $$m$$ and $$n,$$ and $$p.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>We are looking for the quotient of the difference of $$m$$ and  no-selfclose , and $$p.$$ This means we want to divide the difference of $$m$$ and $$n$$ by $$p.$$</p>
        <equation>$$\frac{m - n}{p}$$</equation>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.83">
      <p>Translate the phrase into an algebraic expression: the quotient of the difference of $$a$$ and $$b,$$ and $$cd.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.84">
      <p>Translate the phrase into an algebraic expression: the quotient of the sum of $$p$$ and $$q,$$ and $$r.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Simplify Complex Fractions">
    <p>Our work with fractions so far has included proper fractions, improper fractions, and mixed numbers. Another kind of fraction is called complex fraction, which is a fraction in which the numerator or the denominator contains a fraction.</p>
    <p>Some examples of complex fractions are:</p>
    <equation>$$\frac{\ \frac{6}{7}\ }{\ 3\ }\mspace{32mu}\frac{\ \frac{3}{4}\ }{\ \frac{5}{8}\ }\mspace{32mu}\frac{\ \frac{x}{2}\ }{\ \frac{5}{6}\ }$$</equation>
    <p>To simplify a complex fraction, remember that the fraction bar means division. So the complex fraction $$\frac{\ \frac{3}{4}\ }{\ \frac{5}{8}\ }$$ can be written as $$\frac{3}{4} \div \frac{5}{8}.$$</p>
    <example number="4.43">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\ \frac{3}{4}\ }{\ \frac{5}{8}\ }.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Illustration of the step-by-step process to divide fractions, exemplified by (3/4) / (5/8).">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{\ \frac{3}{4}\ }{\ \frac{5}{8}\ }$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as division.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{3}{4} \div \frac{5}{8}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{3}{4} \cdot \frac{8}{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{3 \cdot 8}{4 \cdot 5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Look for common factors.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{3 \cdot 4\not{} \cdot 2}{4\not{} \cdot 5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Remove common factors and simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{6}{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.85">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\ \frac{2}{3}\ }{\ \frac{5}{6}\ }.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.86">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\ \frac{3}{7}\ }{\ \frac{6}{11}\ }.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="How To">
      <list type="ol" style="1">
        <item>Step 1. 
Rewrite the complex fraction as a division problem.</item>
        <item>Step 2. 
Follow the rules for dividing fractions.</item>
        <item>Step 3. 
Simplify if possible.</item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <example number="4.44">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{- \frac{6}{7}}{3}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step process of dividing the fraction -6/7 by 3, simplifying to -2/7.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{- \frac{6}{7}}{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as division.</cell>
              <cell>$$- \frac{6}{7} \div 3$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second.</cell>
              <cell>$$- \frac{6}{7} \cdot \frac{1}{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply; the product will be negative.</cell>
              <cell>$$- \frac{6 \cdot 1}{7 \cdot 3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Look for common factors.</cell>
              <cell>$$- \frac{3\not{} \cdot 2 \cdot 1}{7 \cdot 3\not{}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Remove common factors and simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$- \frac{2}{7}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.87">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{- \frac{8}{7}}{4}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.88">
      <p>Simplify: $$- \frac{\ 3\ }{\ \frac{9}{10}\ }.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="4.45">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\ \frac{x}{2}\ }{\ \frac{xy}{6}\ }.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a complex rational expression involving variables.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{\ \frac{x}{2}\ }{\ \frac{xy}{6}\ }$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as division.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{x}{2} \div \frac{xy}{6}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{x}{2} \cdot \frac{6}{xy}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{x \cdot 6}{2 \cdot xy}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Look for common factors.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{{x\not{}} \cdot 3 \cdot 2\not{}}{2\not{} \cdot {x\not{}} \cdot y}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Remove common factors and simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{3}{y}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.89">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\ \frac{a}{8}\ }{\ \frac{ab}{6}\ }.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.90">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\ \frac{p}{2}\ }{\ \frac{pq}{8}\ }.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="4.46">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{2\frac{3}{4}}{\frac{1}{8}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step example demonstrating the division of a mixed number by a fraction, including conversion and simplification.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{2\frac{3}{4}}{\frac{1}{8}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as division.</cell>
              <cell>$$2\frac{3}{4} \div \frac{1}{8}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Change the mixed number to an improper fraction.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{11}{4} \div \frac{1}{8}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{11}{4} \cdot \frac{8}{1}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{11 \cdot 8}{4 \cdot 1}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Look for common factors.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{11 \cdot 4\not{} \cdot 2}{4\not{} \cdot 1}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Remove common factors and simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$22$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.91">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\frac{5}{7}}{1\frac{2}{5}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.92">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{\frac{8}{5}}{3\frac{1}{5}}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions with a Fraction Bar">
    <p>Where does the negative sign go in a fraction? Usually, the negative sign is placed in front of the fraction, but you will sometimes see a fraction with a negative numerator or denominator. Remember that fractions represent division. The fraction $$- \frac{1}{3}$$ could be the result of dividing $$\frac{-1}{3},$$ a negative by a positive, or of dividing $$\frac{1}{-3},$$ a positive by a negative. When the numerator and denominator have different signs, the quotient is negative.</p>
    <p>If both the numerator and denominator are negative, then the fraction itself is positive because we are dividing a negative by a negative.</p>
    <equation>$$\frac{-1}{-3} = \frac{1}{3}\mspace{72mu}\frac{\text{negative}}{\text{negative}} = \text{positive}$$</equation>
    <note type="Placement of Negative Sign in a Fraction">
      <p>For any positive numbers $$a$$ and $$b,$$</p>
      <equation>$$\frac{- a}{b} = \frac{a}{- b} = - \frac{a}{b}$$</equation>
    </note>
    <example number="4.47">
      <problem>
        <p>Which of the following fractions are equivalent to $$\frac{7}{-8}?$$</p>
        <equation>$$\ \frac{-7}{-8},\frac{-7}{8},\frac{7}{8}, - \frac{7}{8}$$</equation>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>The quotient of a positive and a negative is a negative, so $$\frac{7}{-8}$$ is negative. Of the fractions listed, $$\ \frac{-7}{8}$$ and $$- \frac{7}{8}$$ are also negative.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.93">
      <p>Which of the following fractions are equivalent to $$\frac{-3}{\ 5}?$$</p>
      <p>$$\ \frac{-3}{-5},\ \frac{3}{5}, - \frac{3}{5},\frac{\ 3}{-5}$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.94">
      <p>Which of the following fractions are equivalent to $$- \frac{2}{7}?$$</p>
      <p>$$\ \frac{-2}{-7},\frac{-2}{7},\frac{2}{7},\frac{2}{-7}$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Fraction bars act as grouping symbols. The expressions above and below the fraction bar should be treated as if they were in parentheses. For example, $$\frac{4 + 8}{5 - 3}$$ means $$(4 + 8) \div (5 - 3).$$ The order of operations tells us to simplify the numerator and the denominator first—as if there were parentheses—before we divide.</p>
    <p>We’ll add fraction bars to our set of grouping symbols from Use the Language of Algebra to have a more complete set here.</p>
    <note type="Grouping Symbols" />
    <note type="How To">
      <list type="ol" style="1">
        <item>Step 1. 
Simplify the numerator.</item>
        <item>Step 2. 
Simplify the denominator.</item>
        <item>Step 3. 
Simplify the fraction.</item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <example number="4.48">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{4 + 8}{5 - 3}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Illustrates the step-by-step simplification of a mathematical fraction.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{4 + 8}{5 - 3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the expression in the numerator.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{12}{5 - 3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the expression in the denominator.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{12}{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the fraction.</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.95">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{4 + 6}{11 - 2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.96">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{3 + 5}{18 - 2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="4.49">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{4 - 2(3)}{2^{2} + 2}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a mathematical expression using the order of operations.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{4 - 2(3)}{2^{2} + 2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the order of operations. Multiply in the numerator and use the exponent in the denominator.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{4 - 6}{4 + 2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the numerator and the denominator.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{-2}{6}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the fraction.</cell>
              <cell>$$- \frac{1}{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.97">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{6 - 3(5)}{3^{2} + 3}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.98">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{4 - 4(6)}{3^{3} + 3}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="4.50">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{{(8 - 4)}^{2}}{8^{2} - 4^{2}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a mathematical fraction using the order of operations, demonstrating the transformation from the initial expression to its final reduced form.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{{(8 - 4)}^{2}}{8^{2} - 4^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the order of operations (parentheses first, then exponents).</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{{(4)}^{2}}{64 - 16}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the numerator and denominator.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{16}{48}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the fraction.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{1}{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.99">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{{(11 - 7)}^{2}}{11^{2} - 7^{2}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="4.100">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{{(6 + 2)}^{2}}{6^{2} + 2^{2}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="4.51">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{4(-3) + 6(-2)}{-3(2)-2}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table shows the step-by-step simplification of a complex rational expression, detailing each operation from multiplication to final division.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{4(-3) + 6(-2)}{-3(2)-2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{-12 + (-12)}{-6 - 2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{-24}{-8}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide.</cell>
              <cell>$$3$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
